- **gates: the archived-report marker gate accepted fewer banner verbs than the docs-health passes write.** Five honestly-bannered archives (EXECUTED/RULED/ROUTED/POINT-IN-TIME forms) failed the nightly doc-annotations gate; the accepted set is widened and anchored to the blockquote+bold banner position so prose bold can never satisfy it (self-test extended with variant + anchoring mutation legs).
- **ci: `check-modsums` ripples closed — the sqliteengine v4.8.0 tag's go.sum update had reached only 3 of 11 engine modules.** All 104 modules tidy; the SC2319 `$?`-after-condition warning in `verify-chain.sh` (failing the lint-scripts job) fixed.

- **decider: `StateCache.Get` now returns a codec-cloned state per caller (owner ruling 2026-11-08, brutal-episode7 B1).** The default Otter cache handed out the LIVE cached instance while concurrent incremental loads folded new events onto it (`decider.loadFromCache`) — a data race and shared-state corruption for pointer/slice/map-shaped states. `Get` now deep-clones through a codec round-trip (the `kv.Cache` isolation pattern, CBOR); a state that cannot round-trip is treated as a miss (the full-load path stays correct) and its entry is invalidated. Pinned by `TestStateCache_ConcurrentGetMutateNoRace` under `-race` plus caller-isolation and uncloneable-miss tests; the `StateCache` interface contract now REQUIRES implementations not to alias internal state.
- **projectionhost: a failed live checkpoint save no longer fails the whole command dispatch (issue #128).** Under concurrent load (24 parallel flows against one sqlite file, Kith CRM) a checkpoint write could hit SQLITE_BUSY and the error climbed out of the live subscriber handler through the in-process bus publish into the dispatch return — a journaled AND applied event answered 500 because its progress marker did not persist, inverting the durability relationship between journal and bookkeeping. The live handler now degrades the failure to a warn; the staging inside the checkpoint flush retries on the next live event and Stop's shutdown flush gives the final retry (the shutdown path already modeled warn-and-continue). At-least-once semantics are unchanged: the reprocessing window widens at most, nothing skips. Pinned by `TestLiveCheckpoint_SaveFailureDoesNotFailHandler`, `TestLiveCheckpoint_SaveFailureFlushesAtShutdown`, and `TestHost_LiveCheckpointFailure_DoesNotStopWorker`.
- **sqliteengine: the production pragmas now hold on EVERY pooled connection, not only the first (issue #128's busy_timeout anomaly).** `sqliteengine.NewFromDSN`/`sqliteengine.NewSQLiteEngineFromDSNWith` applied `PRAGMA busy_timeout=5000` (and `cache_size`) via one `db.ExecContext` after `sql.Open` — per-connection settings that reached exactly one pooled connection. Under `sqliteengine.WithMaxOpenConns` (read-heavy deployments, issue #112) every additional connection opened with busy_timeout=0, so write contention failed IMMEDIATELY with SQLITE_BUSY — reproduced in isolation (a second held-open pooled connection reads busy_timeout=0) and matching #128's "the BUSY surfaced in well under busy_timeout=5000". The pools now open through a connector wrapper over `sqlite.NewConnector` that applies the pragmas in Connect on each new connection; an eager ping keeps bad pragmas failing at construction. Pinned by `TestNewFromDSN_PragmasHoldOnEveryPooledConnection`.
- **doc-check: the gate is live again — the 2026-11-10 module-dir move (0e4fbb3d2) had silently broken every layer of it.** The resolver's `importDir` still trimmed the `/v4` suffix, pointing at versionless directories (`event/`) that no longer hold Go files — every one of 1389 skill references failed as "not found" (702 across the corpus) while the string-pure `importDir` test kept passing; `mirrorAliasDirs` rejected the post-move mirror shape (`event/v4` ↔ `core/v5/event`) as UNKNOWN ambiguity; three test files hardcoded `../..` roots and read nonexistent paths; and both flake invocations (#verify inline + `#doc-check`) passed `../../` file arguments that resolve to `cmd/` since the tool moved to `cmd/doc-check/v4`. Fixed all layers (canonical path keeps the version segment, fullDir keeps the legacy-layout fallback, live-tree regression test `TestFullDir_ResolvesLiveTreeLayout` added), repaired the drift the dead gate had accumulated (`docs/METAENGINE_DOMAIN_LANGUAGE.md` stale `metaengine/adttest` import paths, `system/v4/README.md` unscoped `schema.` alias), and AGENTS.md's documented invocations updated. Full corpus: 2794 references valid across 84 packages.
- **api-stability: `main.go` split under the file-size ratchet — the tool had grown to 430 lines past the 350 ceiling.** The golden-file half (write/verify/diff) moved to `golden.go` in the same package, no signature changes; the newly-live gate chain (file-size is wired into `#verify`, which had been dying earlier at doc-check since the module-dir move) surfaced the committed offender that no completed CI run had caught.

### Changed

- **example: `example/standard-topology` is fully proxy-resolvable — the pre-release `replace` to the local `system` tree died with the `system/v4.18.0` tag (published in the journal-trim wave), and the example now pins the published version directly.** The `check-example-standalone` baseline row is gone (audit back to 0 findings), and the example links the `pgengine` driver alongside sqlite (blank import) so its binary can run the postgres leg of the `cqrs-vm` NixOS test; the layer-gate budget row is 8 → 9 for the second driver.
- **nixos: auto-provisioned postgres uses peer authentication, not the imagined TCP trust — the module now ships the pg_ident/pg_hba wiring that makes its DSNs actually connect.** nixpkgs' default `pg_hba` is `md5` on 127.0.0.1 (passwords the module never had), so the auto-provisioned DSN switched to the unix socket (`postgres:///cqrs_<name>_<engine>?host=/run/postgresql&user=cqrs_<name>_<engine>`) with a scoped `local` hba line and a `cqrs_peer` ident map (`cqrs-<name>` -> `cqrs_<name>_<engine>`): no password ever lands in the store or the rendered config, and the service-user contract is exactly what `cqrs.lib.hardenedService` already sets. Proven on hardware by the `cqrs-vm` NixOS VM test (rendered config, `SELECT 1` as `cqrs-demo` over the socket, `ensureDBOwnership` owner check, wrong-OS-user rejected; nightly CI leg) — the eval pin covers both linux architectures.
