## [metaengine/v4.23.0, metaengine/sqliteengine/v4.8.0, system/v4.18.0 — 2026-11-08 journal-trim + cap-lock-order wave (3 tags)] — 2026-11-08

### Changed

- **metaengine: `AggregateFn` values are whitelisted before SQL interpolation — the exported named-string type was a SQL-injection surface on three engines (2026-11-08 audit).** `pgengine`, `sqliteengine`, and `duckdbengine` interpolated `string(fn)` raw into aggregate SQL while `mysqlengine` whitelisted; any consumer-controlled value (typo or hostile) became SQL. Now `metaengine.IsValidAggregateFn` + `metaengine.RejectInvalidAggregateFn` (classified `metaengine.invalid_aggregate_fn` Rejection wrapping `metaengine.ErrInvalidAggregateFn`) guard every SQL-building aggregate entry on all four engines, explain paths emit a rejection marker instead of hostile SQL, and mysql's silent `default: SUM` fabrication is gone. The cross-engine `adttest.RunAggregateMatrix` harness gains a `HostileFnRejected` pin (injection payload rejected, code asserted, explain marker required) wired into the sqlite, pg, and duckdb engine matrices; package-surface ratchet re-pinned once for the reviewed +3 exports.
- `metaengine` Store batch apply now retries a `Transactional` engine's `RunInTx` up to 4× (jittered backoff) when the commit fails as a transient nothing-committed abort — Dgraph conflict-aborts during projection applies (two consecutive CI-flaked runs, 2026-11-06) no longer fail the apply; any other error still surfaces after one attempt.
- **metaengine: `Store.EnforceCap`/`EnforceCapGrouped` can no longer deadlock against a concurrent fold batch (#123) — the trim now takes the engine transaction BEFORE the query's fold lock.** The pre-fix order (fold lock held across engine scans and deletes) inverted the fold path's fixed order (`dispatchFoldsCoreLocked` opens `RunInTx` — connection first — and takes each fold lock INSIDE it); on a bounded pool (sqlite single-conn setups pin this shape) a fold holding the only connection while wanting the fold lock deadlocked against a trim holding the fold lock while wanting a connection — an order-dependent permanent hang, observed in webphone's projection host as 60-600s stalls (~1-in-2 under a contacts+transcripts battery). The trim now runs inside the engine's `RunInTx` with the fold lock taken inside (`withCapTrimTx`; non-transactional engines keep the plain fold lock, whose holders never wait on a pool), which also makes each trim atomic per engine instead of per-row deletes. Pinned by `sqliteengine.TestSQLiteEnforceCapDoesNotDeadlockAgainstFolds`: pre-fix FAIL (90s watchdog, both goroutines parked in `WaitGroup.Wait`), post-fix green.

### Added

- **system: the ADR-0165 ladder is pinned shut across layers — the Go, CUE, and NixOS standard-topology presets assert ONE shared instance triple.** `system/testdata/standard-instance-triple.json` is asserted by the `system.Standard` suite, the `#check-cue-config` gate (`standard.instances` diff, 6-fault self-test), and the new `cqrs-nixos-eval` flake check (pure `nixosSystem` evaluation on x86_64-linux AND aarch64-linux, wired into CI); cross-layer round-trip tests load the CUE golden and the NixOS-rendered deployment JSON back through `system.LoadConfig` verbatim, so no preset twin can drift from what operators actually boot. The `#check-cue-config` gate is wired into `#verify`/`#verify-fast` and ci.yml. `example/standard-topology` is the runnable demo of the whole ladder (engines-only config → `system.Standard` → sqlite → dispatch → Lookup fold; registered in the example gates incl. the standalone-audit baseline until the system tag wave publishes `Standard`).
- **system: `FuzzLoadConfigJSON` (117th fuzz target, NixOS-rendered artifact in the seed corpus) and rapid properties pin `system.Standard` idempotence + engine-name-permutation invariance.**
- **system: the standard deployment topology is one call now — `system.Standard(d)` stamps the canonical three-instance shape (ADR-0165): WAL (`source-of-truth`) + dedicated `queries` audit-log instance on the WAL engine ("primary" when declared, else the sorted-first name), and a `projections` instance pooling every declared engine for the cost-based planner.** A pure data filler, not a builder: configs that already declare instances (or declare no engines) pass through unchanged, and engines/buses/durability/priorities stay operator-owned — the Go-code `stack` presets ADR-0123 killed hid deployment inside bundles; `Standard` inspects and completes operator data. `system.LoadConfig` also reads JSON now (`.json` extension, stdlib decode, zero new deps), which makes `cue export` output loadable verbatim. The format ladder is completed by two non-Go artifacts: `system/config.cue` (the DeploymentConfig schema with the standard topology as CUE defaults — authoring surface only, NO runtime CUE dependency, per the ADR-0163 verdict on CUE tooling) and the first-party `nixosModules.cqrs` NixOS module (`cqrs.deployments.<name>` renders the JSON to `/etc/cqrs/<name>/deployment.json`, fills the standard topology, and auto-provisions postgres DBs+users / sqlite StateDirectory files when an engine has no explicit DSN; `cqrs.lib.hardenedService` supplies the systemd fragment — the service unit stays app-owned). Drift-pinned by `nix run .#check-cue-config` (cue vet + golden preset eval + bidirectional koanf-tag comparison, `--self-test` included).
- `metaengine.TransientCommitError` + `metaengine.IsTransientCommit`: the retriable-as-a-whole commit-abort marker a transactional engine attaches when a commit conflict-aborted with NOTHING committed; `metaengine/dgraphengine` marks its conflict-aborted `RunInTx` commits with it.
- **metaengine + sqliteengine + system: journal trimming exists — `TrimStream` (GDPR erasure) and `TrimJournalBefore` (age sweep), issue #118.** Two optional engine capabilities (`metaengine.StreamTrimmer`, `metaengine.JournalSweeper`) plus the `System.TrimStream(ref)` / `System.TrimJournalBefore(cutoff)` maintenance surfaces. `TrimStream` deletes one stream's journal rows AND its snapshot row — co-trimmed in the SAME transaction under the caller's snapshot collection (a surviving snapshot would resurrect trimmed state on replay) — leaving the stream never-written (a fresh append at expected version 0 succeeds). `TrimJournalBefore` deletes every typed `occurred_at` row older than the cutoff plus the snapshots of the streams it swept (legacy NULL-timestamp rows are conservatively kept; the snapshot subquery resolves against the pre-sweep rows inside the one transaction). sqliteengine implements both; the memory engine implements `TrimStream` (in-process maps, no snapshots to co-trim; `TrimJournalBefore` answers `system.ErrTrimUnavailable`). Operator-invoked maintenance, never read/write-path: doctrine stays deletion-as-domain-event (ADR-0114) at the event level; this is the byte-erasure layer below, where webphone's retention gate ("delete actually deletes" — journal + projection + view) needs exactly these two calls. Pinned end-to-end by `system/trim_test.go` (trim → empty load → fresh re-append; sweep boundary; snapshot co-trim through the REAL system surface) and engine-side by `sqliteengine/stream_trim_test.go` (mixed ages, NULL kept, per-stream snapshot fates).
- **sqliteengine: opt-in expression index for meta_map sort keys — `sqliteengine.WithSortIndex(collection, path, desc)` (issue #122): sorted and keyset-paginated standard scans stop paying a full-collection scan + temp B-tree per page.** Every standard `meta_map` scan orders by `json_extract(value, '$.field')`; with no supporting index each page sorted the whole collection (measured 58.5ms per 61-row page at 100k rows; the same class as nsfw-classifier's `/history` board, linear in collection size). The option emits `CREATE INDEX IF NOT EXISTS idx_map_sort_<collection>_<field> ON meta_map(collection, json_extract(value, path) [DESC], key)` — the trailing `key` mirrors the scans' ORDER BY tiebreak (without it the planner keeps the PK scan plus a temp B-tree), and the index is deliberately NOT partial (a `WHERE json_valid(value)` index is unusable for scans that do not imply the predicate; meta_map values are engine-encoded JSON by construction). Planner adoption pinned by `EXPLAIN QUERY PLAN` (`TestWithSortIndex_PlannerUsesIndex` — SEARCH ... USING INDEX, no temp B-tree), row-identity with and without the index across full keyset pagination (`TestWithSortIndex_ScanResultsIdentical`), construction idempotency, and declaration validation. Consumer-owned cost decision: every row rewrite maintains the index, which is why it is opt-in per collection rather than a default. A collection migrated to a planned table simply ignores the index — the option holds no state that breaks `ApplyLayout`.

## [metaengine/v4.22.0, metaengine/sqliteengine/v4.7.1, system/v4.17.0 — 2026-11-06 MaxOpenConns plumbing wave (3 tags)] — 2026-11-06
- **system + metaengine + sqliteengine: the pool ceiling is deployment config now — `system.EngineConfig.MaxOpenConns` flows through `metaengine.DriverConfig.MaxOpenConns` to the sqlite driver's `sqliteengine.WithMaxOpenConns` (issue #112 follow-up, the Kith read-storm class).** `system.New`'s `EngineConfig` gains a `max_open_conns` koanf field (YAML/TOML operators raise the ceiling without code); `metaengine.DriverConfig` carries it to every registered driver; the sqlite driver factory applies it only when raised (>0) — the serialized `MaxOpenConns(1)` default stays deliberate, and engines without a pool ignore the field. Pinned by the driver-factory test proving `sqliteengine.DBStats` reports 1 by default and the raised ceiling when set. This closes the plumbing gap that kept `sqliteengine.WithMaxOpenConns` constructor-only (composition-root deployments could not reach it).


## [catalog/v4.13.0, kv/v4.4.0, id/v4.7.5, metaengine/v4.21.0, metaengine/sqliteengine/v4.7.0, cmd/cqrs-lint/v4.21.0, systemscenario/v4.3.0 — 2026-11-06 post-sweep issue wave (7 tags)] — 2026-11-06

### Changed

- **metaengine: `Store.Verify`/`Store.Backfill` failures are exported, classified and branchable — "Verify is a structural no-op here" is no longer indistinguishable from real drift (issue #116).** Every way `Verify` can end is now a matchable sentinel carrying an error family: `metaengine.ErrNoEventLog` and `metaengine.ErrNoQueryDecls` (Rejection — retrying cannot help, the deployment has to change) versus `metaengine.ErrCollectionCountMismatch` and `metaengine.ErrVerifyDrift` (Corruption — the projection state no longer follows from its journal; drift stays wrapped with the live/replay row counts). Health and doctor code branches with `errors.Is` instead of hardcoding the knowledge that Verify is a no-op under `system.New`, and the plain-`errors.New` default-Transient classification stops making a structurally impossible Verify look retryable. Sibling fix: `Store.Backfill` returns `metaengine.ErrNoEventLog` when no log is attached instead of reporting success for work it never did (an attached-but-empty log is still a genuine nil), and the new `metaengine.Store.EventLogAttached` lets a caller ask the question without provoking the error. `system.VerifyProjections` documents that the composition root attaches no event log, so with a projection store configured it returns `metaengine.ErrNoEventLog` by construction.
- **Turso IVM verified range extended to v0.8.3 (pin catch-up).** `metaengine.TursoGoIVMVerifiedThrough` advances v0.8.2 → v0.8.3 and `TursoGoIVMLastVerified` to 2026-11-05: the workspace go.mod pins rode the 11-04 dependency sweep to tursogo v0.8.3 ahead of the constant, and the `-tags ivmrepro` repro suite re-ran GREEN on the new pin — all three grouped-view defects (A/B/C) still reproduce (defect C at the full 27k-row wall, 164s), so the operator caveat range extends one version with no behavior change (scalar views remain exact). `#check-turso-version` green across all live citations and pins after the six-site sweep (FEATURES, gotchas, ADR-0135, bench doc, readmodels, recipes).

### Added

- **metaengine: `metaengine.WithPrefetch(cache)` — the scan option cqrs-lint's F026 has been recommending since it shipped (issue #115), plus the cache key it needed to be correct.** `PrefetchCache` existed with only a reader-level attach point (`NewReader[V](...).WithPrefetch(cache)`), so a reader built per request — the HTTP-handler shape — could not opt into caching, and the F026 finding suggested a `metaengine.WithPrefetch(n)` that no release shipped (points at the wrong shape) while its detection missed the real reader-level `WithPrefetch` method form, so a correctly-configured project was flagged anyway. Now: the per-call option caches for that call (an explicit `WithPrefetch(nil)` turns caching off even when the reader has a default), and `metaengine.PrefetchCache.Len` is the "is this cache actually used" handle. The cache key is **shape-qualified** — collection + filters + sort + cursor — because the old collection+cursor key could serve a page cached for one filter/sort to a scan of another shape (silent wrong rows); the read now resolves after range/IN expansion so the read shape and the write shape are identical. cqrs-lint F026 is fixed alongside: it resolves import aliases (`metaeng "…/metaengine/v4"` is seen), accepts BOTH attach forms, only coaches CURSOR-paginated scans (the old gate fired on ANY `Scan`, and a one-shot scan pays the cache's doubled fetch for no benefit — which is why `example/taskmanager` was falsely flagged), and its suggestion names the reader form (available in every v4 release) and the option with its minimum version (metaengine v4.21.0). Package-surface ratchet re-pinned once for the reviewed shift (metaengine 836 → 841 exports, 168 → 169 production files — `PrefetchCache` moved to its own file to keep `dx.go` inside the line ratchet); `metaengine.ErrNoEventLog` and friends are named by tests, so no new untested-export debt.
- **metaengine: per-group projection caps — `metaengine.WithCapPerGroup(groupCol, perGroupRows)` (issue #114, ADR-0164): retention rules per KEY GROUP are a declaration now, not a hand-rolled trim loop.** Rides on a `WithCap` declaration: each distinct group value keeps its newest perGroupRows rows (WithCap's orderBy orders survivors within groups) while WithCap's maxRows stays the collection-global ceiling — one owner's flood can no longer evict another owner's rows (the per-owner transcript/quota class). Enforcement composes: `metaengine.Store.EnforceCap` runs the grouped pass then the global pass under one fold lock, `metaengine.Store.EnforceCapGrouped` trims only the grouped bound, `metaengine.Store.EnforceCaps` reaches both. Engines implementing `metaengine.CapGroupTrimmer` trim every group in one window-function DELETE (sqliteengine: `ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY …) … RETURNING key`, real columns on planned tables, `json_extract` on meta_map; turso inherits via delegation) and the returned keys notify SSE subscribers; engines without the capability — and cell-versioned engines, whose history a bulk delete would bypass — return `metaengine.ErrCapGroupPushdownUnsupported` and fall back to the store's portable Go scan (identical observable contract, pinned by `metaengine/adttest.AssertGroupedCapContract` across memory, sqlite meta_map/planned/versioned, and turso; `docs/engine-capabilities.md` gains the capability column). Declaration validation is fail-closed: requires the WithCap base, a group field on the result type (field name or json tag, case-insensitive — the same matching `ExtractFields` applies), and plain-identifier columns (they flow into engine SQL); `metaengine.QueryConfig.CapGroupCol`/`CapGroupRows` expose the declaration. The grouped declaration also resolves type-ambiguous keys: the group column is excluded from key-field candidates, so string-key + string-group result types extract their key unambiguously (a plain cap over such a shape stays rejected — there is nothing to exclude).
- **sqliteengine: `WithMaxOpenConns` — the pool-ceiling escape hatch is an option now (issue #112 follow-up, owner-ruled 2026-11-05).** `sqliteengine.WithMaxOpenConns(n)` raises the DSN constructors' deliberate `MaxOpenConns(1)` serialized default for read-heavy deployments (the Kith read-storm class): WAL supports concurrent readers, so read-dominated deployments stop paying one-long-scan-blocks-everything per query; writes then queue behind `busy_timeout` retries instead of the pool. Works with `NewSQLiteEngineFromDSNWith`, `NewSQLiteEngine`, and `NewFromDSN` (which gains a backward-compatible `...EngineOption` variadic); `n <= 0` keeps the default (the database/sql meaning of 0 — unlimited — is a footgun behind an option named "max"). `sqliteengine.DBStats(eng)` exposes the owned handle's pool statistics so owning-constructor callers can verify the ceiling took effect.
- **open-issue sweep (2026-11-04): 13 consumer-filed gaps closed across catalog, kv, cqrs-lint, sqliteengine, docserver, and id (issues #96 #92 #87 #90 #88 #86 #94 #97 #93 #91 #95 #89 #102 #111 #113 #109 #110 #101 #104).** catalog: `catalog.Channels` sets the message-to-channel half of the association (message pages render their channel back-links; both directions feed the AsyncAPI export); `catalog.WithTypedParam` declares integer/boolean/enum parameters (rendered with type+enum in OpenAPI) instead of the untyped string `catalog.WithParam` emits; `catalog.WithResponseHeader` types header contracts such as 429 + Retry-After into `ResponseSpec.Headers`, rendered as OpenAPI headers; `catalog.WithRequestBodyContent` (+ `Message.RequestBodyMediaType`/`RequestBodySchema`) emits multipart/form-data request bodies, previously unrepresentable; `openapi.WithOpenAPIVersion` opts a document into 3.1.0 (default stays 3.0.3); `simple.Builder.AddChannel`/`AddDataStore` complete the facade's resource set; the AsyncAPI exporter honors catalog-declared Channels (address, name, delivery guarantee; synthetic fallback unchanged for channel-less catalogs); nameless messages (e.g. `Query[struct{}]`) get their display name derived from the ID at build; `caseutil.ToPascal` treats dots as word separators so dotted IDs produce codegen-clean operationIds; `camelCaseToHuman` keeps initialism runs whole (External ID Set Payload, not External I D Set Payload); `docserver.Config.SkipIndexRoute` lets consumers own `GET <prefix>`. kv: `kv.NewCacheForViewStore` fronts any `kv.ViewStore` (not only `*kv.TypedStore`) with the TinyLFU cache — shallow-copy isolation by default, `kv.WithCacheCopyFunc` for reference-bearing views, `Has` falls back to Get — deleting the hand-rolled adapters SQL view stores forced. cqrs-lint: `--update-baseline` re-attaches reasons orphaned by line shifts (unambiguous same-(rule,file) rematch) and WARNS on every reason it must drop (the crm 6-of-60 silent wipe); a recorded scorecard waiver now also suppresses its module's adoption finding (otel/F003 through listing/F016) into `--show-suppressed` carrying the waiver reason + trigger; `scorecard.modules` pins in `.cqrs-lint.json` mark composition-root-wired modules used (`analyzer.ApplyModulePins`, project config wins, false pins rejected in favor of waivers) — config-pins-beat-detection closes the invisible-composition-root class (consumers wiring the host through the system root's `ProjectionHost()` accessor). sqliteengine: both DSN constructors set `PRAGMA cache_size=-65536` (64 MiB) before caller pragmas — the 2 MB default page cache thrashed scan-heavy deployments (measured 8.16 GB reads/43 min against a 536 MB journal); `MaxOpenConns(1)` serialization is now documented as deliberate with the raise-it escape hatch. id: the package doc states the bare-value keying contract (persist `Get()`, never `String()` — the 26-day Standup-Killer outage class) with a KEYING note on `StreamID` itself.
- **cqrs-upgrade: `--to-v5` — the v5 import rewrite is a command now (T08 slice 1, ADR-0152).** `cqrs-upgrade --to-v5 [--dry-run] [--workspace] <dir>` rewrites imports of the folded v4 trains (`event`, `command`, `query`, `id` + `idtest`, `kv` + `viewstoretest`, `record`, `dedup`, `dispatcher`, `metadata`, `commandtest`, `querytest`) to their `core/v5` twins. The rewrite is AST-discovered and byte-exact: only quoted literals inside real import specs change, so v4 paths inside strings, comments, or prose are never touched (alias/blank/underscore imports preserved; unit-pinned, plus idempotency and dry-run-writes-nothing pins). `go.mod` stays report-only in this slice — `core/v5` is untagged, so an applied require could not resolve; the report names the trains that must move. The compiled-in fold table is drift-gated against the actual `core/v5` tree (`TestV5TableMatchesCoreTree`): extending the fold without extending the table fails the suite. `--strict --to-v5` fails on errored modules first (unscanned is unproven), then on any not-yet-folded cqrs v4 import — the fleet can gate the migration exactly like v5-removed API usage. Dry-run receipts on the three flagship consumers: cqrs-htmx 29 modules / 1,413 files / 1,000 planned rewrites (32 trains still to fold), go-appkit 12/308/84 (13), bank-sync 5/459/137 (20) — evidence: `docs/evidence/2026-11-04_t08-tov5-dry-run-receipts.md`.
- **repo: the README quick-start is compile-verified, and the canonical-facts gate now sees backticked counts (T06).** (a) `cmd/doc-check/readme_compile_test.go` compiles the four README quick-start fences as ONE narrative program (domain + query declared, `system.New` booted, command dispatched, view read) against the workspace — the same machinery that compile-verifies `recipes.md`, applied to the repo's front door; a fence ratchet (`TestReadmeFencesClassified`) fails on any added/removed/renamed fence, so an API drift now breaks the quick-start HERE instead of in a consumer's editor. (b) `check-canonical-facts.sh` strips backticks before the go.mod-count regex — prose like "103 `go.mod` files" was invisible to the gate and could rot freely (the 22-15 session fixed drifted backticked counts BY HAND; two stale meta-sentences claiming the blindness was permanent are corrected in AGENTS.md and the v5 playbook); the self-test gains two mutation legs (wrong backticked count caught, honest backticked count passes). Sweep result: all five DOC_SET files carry honest backticked counts today.
- **cqrs-lint: T009 `hand-booted-system-in-tests` — consumer-side systemscenario coaching, closing the test-framework coverage map.** `testrules.NewT009Detector` fires when a test file boots a real system via `system.New` but the project never imports `systemscenario` (any import satisfies — `System` for fresh boots, `Adopt` for wrapper facades that own the boot), anchored at the hand-boot call where the migration lands. This is the consumer-side twin of the library's own `check-systemscenario-adoption.sh` gate (ADR-0153): the script + its reasoned allowlist owns self-lint, the rule owns consumers, so the rule registers under consumer-coaching (library self-lint exempt) and consumers with a deliberate lower-level boot suppress with `//cqrs-lint:ignore(T009) <reason>`. Fires ONLY when a test actually boots — projects whose tests never call `system.New` owe no harness. Completes the test-framework coverage map: `scenario` (T001 deciders / T002 projections), `eventtest` (T003), `systemscenario` (T009) are coached; `benchkit` and `testutil` stay deliberately uncoached (benchkit is EXPERIMENTAL with CLI-at-arm's-length as the documented preference; testutil is an internal conformance utility — both excluded from the module catalog with reasons). An attempted `eventtest` scorecard entry was rejected by the catalog's own hint-ambiguity guard and stays out: it is a package inside `event/v4`, not an adoptable module — the scorecard tracks modules (the stale DefaultCatalog count comment was corrected to the real 7 core / 36 scored on sight). F032 interplay is by design: test-framework-only consumers never owe catalog documentation (F032 scans non-test files). Wiring: consumer-coaching registry, rule catalog, RULES.md regenerated (217 rules), detector-count meta-test re-pinned, README counts, api-stability golden (+1 export); `example/taskmanager` already imports systemscenario so both goldens are untouched.
- **testing: the coverage floor now covers EVERY module and every package, not 11 of 89.** `scripts/coverage-baseline.txt` pins all 89 `testModules` entries and `scripts/coverage-package-baseline.txt` pins all 168 packages; `check-coverage.sh` measures one workspace-wide coverprofile (aggregate statements per module, deepest module dir wins) instead of the first package's coverage line, and fails on a regression beyond tolerance, a module with no baseline row, a dangling row, a package that lost its tests, or coverage data vanishing. `--update` is the only way to move a number, `--report` writes the CI artifacts (`module-coverage.tsv`, `pkg-coverage.tsv`, `coverage-report.md`), and `--self-test` fault-injects nine cases. Measured 2026-10-26: decider 95.8, storage/memory 95.7, record 97.4, commandlifecycle 96.1 down to `queue/postgres` 0.2 (integration-gated) — the aggregate numbers differ from the old per-first-package ones by design.
- **testing: exported API must be named by a test, or be frozen debt.** `scripts/check-untested-exports.sh` reads `docs/api_surface.txt` (6,334 symbols) and fails when a symbol is named by NO test file and is not on `scripts/untested-exports-baseline.txt` (1,088 rows, 17.2%, shrink-only; `--update` preserves a row's `# reason`). A name-based ratchet, deliberately: it proves a promise is exercised somewhere, not that the assertion is strong — the zero-assertion class is mutation's job (TODO [T10]).
